abel=3D"Toggle more/less fact data"><span class=3D"link-blue">(more)</span>=
</button></span></div></figcaption></figure></div></div><p class=3D"topic-p=
aragraph">Food serves multiple functions in most living organisms. For exam=
ple, it provides materials that are metabolized to supply the <a href=3D"ht=
tps://www.britannica.com/science/energy" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-=
preview=3D"true">energy</a> required for the absorption and translocation o=
f <span id=3D"ref414465"></span><a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/scien=
ce/nutrient" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">nutrients</=
a>, for the synthesis of <a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/cell=
-biology" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">cell</a> mater=
ials, for movement and <a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/topic/locomoti=
on" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">locomotion</a>, for =
<a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/excretion" class=3D"md-crossl=
ink " data-show-preview=3D"true">excretion</a> of waste products, and for a=
ll other activities of the organism. Food also provides materials from whic=
h all the structural and <span id=3D"ref414493"></span><a href=3D"https://w=
ww.britannica.com/science/catalysis" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-prev=
iew=3D"true">catalytic</a> components of the living cell can be assembled. =
Living organisms differ in the particular substances that they require as f=
ood, in the manner in which they synthesize food substances or obtain them =
from the surrounding <a class=3D"md-dictionary-link md-dictionary-tt-off mw=
" data-term=3D"environment" href=3D"https://www.merriam-webster.com/diction=
ary/environment" data-type=3D"MW">environment</a>, and in the functions tha=
t these substances carry out in their cells. Nevertheless, general patterns=
 can be discerned in the nutritional process throughout the living world an=
d in the types of nutrients that are required to sustain <a href=3D"https:/=
/www.britannica.com/science/life" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=
=3D"true">life</a>. These patterns are the subject of this article. For a f=
ull discussion of the nutritional requirements of humans in particular, <em=
>see</em> the article <a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/human-n=
utrition" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">nutrition, hum=
an</a>.</p><!--[MOD2]--><span class=3D"marker MOD2 mod-inline"></span></sec=
tion>     <!--[H2]--><span class=3D"marker h2"></span><div class=3D"md-sent=
inel--spy-target" bis_skin_checked=3D"1"></div><section data-level=3D"1" id=
=3D"ref35933" data-has-spy=3D"true"><h2 class=3D"h1"><span id=3D"ref414472"=
></span><a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/nutritional-type" cla=
ss=3D"md-crosslink ">Nutritional</a> patterns in the living world</h2> <!--=
[PREMOD3]--><span class=3D"marker PREMOD3 mod-inline"></span><p class=3D"to=
pic-paragraph">Living organisms can be categorized by the way in which the =
functions of food are carried out in their bodies. Thus, organisms such as =
green <a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/plant/plant" class=3D"md-crossl=
ink " data-show-preview=3D"true">plants</a> and some <a href=3D"https://www=
.britannica.com/science/bacteria" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=
=3D"true">bacteria</a> that need only <a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com=
/science/inorganic-compound" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"t=
rue">inorganic compounds</a> for <a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/scie=
nce/growth-biology" class=3D"md-crosslink autoxref " data-show-preview=3D"t=
rue">growth</a> can be called <span id=3D"ref414474"></span><a href=3D"http=
s://www.britannica.com/science/autotroph" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show=
-preview=3D"true">autotrophic</a> organisms; and organisms, including all <=
a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/animal/animal" class=3D"md-crosslink "=
 data-show-preview=3D"true">animals</a>, <a href=3D"https://www.britannica.=
com/science/fungus" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">fung=
i</a>, and most bacteria, that require both inorganic and <a href=3D"https:=
//www.britannica.com/science/organic-compound" class=3D"md-crosslink " data=
-show-preview=3D"true">organic compounds</a> for growth are called <span id=
=3D"ref414475"></span><a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/heterot=
roph" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">heterotrophic</a>.=
 Other classifications have been used to include various other nutritional =
patterns. In one scheme, organisms are classified according to the energy s=
ource they utilize. Phototrophic, or <span id=3D"ref414476"></span><a href=
=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/photosynthesis" class=3D"md-crosslin=
k " data-show-preview=3D"true">photosynthetic</a>, organisms trap <a href=
=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/light" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-=
show-preview=3D"true">light</a> energy and convert it to <a href=3D"https:/=
/www.britannica.com/science/chemical-energy" class=3D"md-crosslink autoxref=
 " data-show-preview=3D"true">chemical energy</a>, whereas <span id=3D"ref4=
14478"></span><a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/chemotroph" cla=
ss=3D"md-crosslink ">chemoautotrophic</a>, or chemosynthetic, organisms uti=
lize inorganic or organic <a class=3D"md-dictionary-link md-dictionary-tt-o=
ff mw" data-term=3D"compounds" href=3D"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dict=
ionary/compounds" data-type=3D"MW">compounds</a> to supply their energy req=
uirements. If the <a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/electron" c=
lass=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">electron</a>-donor materi=
als utilized to form reduced <a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/=
coenzyme" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">coenzymes</a> =
consist of inorganic compounds, the organism is said to be <span id=3D"ref4=
14479"></span><a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/lithotroph" cla=
ss=3D"md-crosslink ">lithotrophic</a>; if organic, the organism is <span id=
=3D"ref414480"></span>organotrophic.</p><!--[MOD3]--><span class=3D"marker =
MOD3 mod-inline"></span> <!--[PREMOD4]--><span class=3D"marker PREMOD4 mod-=
inline"></span><p class=3D"topic-paragraph">Combinations of these patterns =
may also be used to describe organisms. Higher plants, for example, are <sp=
an id=3D"ref414481"></span>photolithotrophic; i.e., they utilize light ener=
gy, with the inorganic <a class=3D"md-dictionary-link md-dictionary-tt-off =
mw" data-term=3D"compound" href=3D"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictiona=
ry/compound" data-type=3D"MW">compound</a> <a href=3D"https://www.britannic=
a.com/science/water" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">wat=
er</a> serving as the ultimate electron donor. Certain photosynthetic bacte=
ria that cannot utilize water as the electron donor and require organic com=
pounds for this purpose are called <span id=3D"ref414482"></span>photoorgan=
otrophs. Animals, according to this classification, are <span id=3D"ref4144=
83"></span>chemoorganotrophs; i.e., they utilize <a href=3D"https://www.bri=
tannica.com/science/chemical-compound" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-pr=
eview=3D"true">chemical compounds</a> to supply energy and organic compound=
s as electron donors.</p><div class=3D"js-qf-module qf-module px-40 px-sm-2=
