EMOD5]--><span class=3D"marker PREMOD5 mod-inline"></span><p class=3D"topic=
-paragraph">Despite wide variations in the nature of the external energy so=
urce utilized by various organisms, all organisms form from their external =
energy source an immediate source of energy, the chemical compound <span id=
=3D"ref414477"></span><a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/adenosi=
ne-triphosphate" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">adenosi=
ne triphosphate</a> (ATP). This energy-rich compound is common to all cells=
. Through the breaking of its high-energy <a href=3D"https://www.britannica=
.com/science/phosphate" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">=
phosphate</a> bonds and thus by its <a class=3D"md-dictionary-link md-dicti=
onary-tt-off eb" data-term=3D"conversion" href=3D"https://www.britannica.co=
m/dictionary/conversion" data-type=3D"EB">conversion</a> to a less energy-r=
ich compound, adenosine diphosphate (ADP), ATP provides the energy for the =
chemical and mechanical work required by an organism. The energy requiremen=
ts of organisms can be measured in either <a href=3D"https://www.britannica=
.com/science/joule" class=3D"md-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">joul=
es</a> or <a href=3D"https://www.britannica.com/science/calorie" class=3D"m=
d-crosslink " data-show-preview=3D"true">calories</a>.</p><a class=3D"link-=
